Done! Here's the summary:

### What changed

`ReportGenerator` now takes `TimeProvider` via a primary constructor and calls `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()` instead of `DateTime.UtcNow`.

### How to test with deterministic time

Install the testing helper in your test project:

```
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then in your tests:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 3, 15, 10, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

Assert.Contains("20260315_100000", path);

// You can also advance time:
fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(2));
```

`TimeProvider` is built into .NET 10 — no additional production dependency needed. `FakeTimeProvider` gives you full control over time in tests.
